      "content": "The debate centers on whether \"gender\" (a person's internal sense of identity) should be the sole criterion for fair competition or if \"biological sex\" (specifically pre-pubertal or reproductive anatomy) must be a decisive factor. Proponents of the identity argument argue that legal and ethical standards for decades have protected transgender people from discrimination, and that a person should not be denied the right to compete based on a \"real\" physical trait they also cannot control.\n\nConversely, opponents emphasize the \"fairness\" argument, which asserts that for elite division female categories, some anti- androgenic effects of modern society on transgender women creates an inherent competitive imbalance when compared to fully cisgender women. \"Proper\" and \"decent\" sports often lead to the creation of \"high\" and \"low\" gender categories to accommodate these legitimate concerns without the \"real\" \"real\" \"decent\" \"decent\" \"real\" \"real\" \"decent\" \"decent\" \"real\" \"real\" \"decent\" \"decent\" \"real\" \"real\" \"decent\" \"decent\" \"real\" \"real\" \"decent\" \"decent\" \"real\"",
